WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.252
Preventing path traversal in C depends on
explicit file access policy,

2
00:00:04.332 --> 00:00:06.208
not clever string filtering alone.

3
00:00:06.288 --> 00:00:09.164
The application should know which roots
are approved,

4
00:00:09.244 --> 00:00:12.309
which workflows may use them, which names
are acceptable,

5
00:00:12.389 --> 00:00:14.452
and which file operations are allowed.

6
00:00:14.532 --> 00:00:17.909
Once that policy is clear, the code can
enforce it consistently.

7
00:00:17.989 --> 00:00:21.991
The strongest basic pattern is trusted
root plus constrained name plus safe

8
00:00:22.071 --> 00:00:22.884
construction.

9
00:00:22.964 --> 00:00:26.403
The root comes from trusted configuration
or application state.

10
00:00:26.483 --> 00:00:30.110
The untrusted input is reduced to a safe
relative name or identifier.

11
00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:34.255
The path is built with a reviewed helper,
resolved or checked as appropriate,

12
00:00:34.335 --> 00:00:38.837
and confirmed to remain inside the
approved area before the file operation proceeds.

13
00:00:38.917 --> 00:00:42.794
External filenames, archive entries,
metadata names, uploaded labels,

14
00:00:42.874 --> 00:00:46.939
generated report parts, and
database-derived names should all be treated as

15
00:00:47.019 --> 00:00:49.583
untrusted until they match the workflow's
rule.

16
00:00:49.663 --> 00:00:53.790
A value does not become safe because it
came through another file or because the

17
00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:55.683
application generated part of it.

18
00:00:55.763 --> 00:00:59.078
Each path component needs a clear source
and validation story.

19
00:00:59.158 --> 00:01:01.972
Path checks should be combined with least
privilege,

20
00:01:02.052 --> 00:01:06.179
safe open behavior, permissions,
race-aware workflows, and narrow cleanup.

21
00:01:06.259 --> 00:01:09.323
These layers help when one part of the
design is imperfect.

22
00:01:09.403 --> 00:01:13.655
They also make the file boundary easier to
reason about because the application,

23
00:01:13.735 --> 00:01:17.862
the operating system, and the directory
layout all reinforce the same intent.

24
00:01:17.942 --> 00:01:22.070
Finally, keep the prevention logic simple,
centralized, and easy to inspect.

25
00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:23.963
Tests should cover failure cases.

26
00:01:24.043 --> 00:01:27.045
Static analysis and warnings should be
taken seriously.

27
00:01:27.125 --> 00:01:30.939
Reviews should trace every user-influenced
path into file operations.

28
00:01:31.019 --> 00:01:33.145
Traversal-resistant code is not magic;

29
00:01:33.225 --> 00:01:37.540
it is disciplined boundary design applied
every time a path meets the file system.
